"<strong>Could it have liquid water?</strong> The starlight a planet gets, compared with Earth, is the star’s luminosity \\(L\\) (its power, Sun = {#}) divided by \\(a^2\\). For stars near the Sun’s mass a rough rule is \\(L \\approx M_s^4\\). A planet like Earth could keep liquid water if it gets between about {#} and {#} times Earth’s starlight: that is the habitable zone (Kopparapu and others, {#}, for a Sun like star). With no greenhouse and an albedo like Earth’s, a planet’s temperature is about {#} K × (starlight compared with Earth)\\(^{1/4}\\); the power {#}/{#} is the square root taken twice.",
"<strong>Step {#}, starlight and temperature:</strong> luminosity \\(L\\) = star mass to the fourth power (\\(M_s \\times M_s \\times M_s \\times M_s\\)); starlight = \\(L\\) ÷ \\(a^2\\), with \\(a\\) from step {#}; temperature = {#} K × √(√(starlight)).",
